            <h3>Seasonal Variations</h3>

            <p>Pricing and availability oscillate predictably across the calendar, shaped by British tourism patterns, American vacation habits, and Pittsburgh's university schedules.</p>
            <h3>Peak Season: June Through August</h3>

            <p>Summer transforms London into a global magnet for transatlantic visitors.</p>
            <p><b>Market dynamics:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Economy round-trips climb to $900-$1,800</li>
                <li>Premium cabins reach $3,000-$5,500</li>
                <li>British Airways nonstop operates at maximum frequency</li>
                <li>Booking of 2-3 months advisable for optimal pricing</li>
                <li>Hotel inventory in central London compresses significantly</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Premium expense periods:</h3>

            <ul>
                <li>Late June through early July - Wimbledon and summer concert season</li>
                <li>August - British holiday month with domestic travel surge</li>
                <li>US Independence Day week - American demand spike</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Shoulder Windows: April to May, September to October</h3>

            <p>These intervals reward flexible travelers with exceptional value.</p>
            <p><b>Why shoulders excel:</b></p>
            <ul>
                <li>Fares retreat 20-35% from summer peaks</li>
                <li>Temperatures pleasant for London exploration</li>
                <li>Crowds thin at the British Museum, Tower of London, and Westminster</li>
                <li>Ideal for cultural tourism and theater attendance</li>
                <li>Pitt and CMU semester breaks create brief demand spikes</li>
            </ul>
            <h3>Low Season: November Through March</h3>

            <p>Winter presents budget opportunities alongside distinctly British atmospheric charm.</p>
            <h3>Financial advantages:</h3>

